  {
    "n": 5,
    "mode": "native",
    "native": "<div class=\"slide-native sm-skill sm-check\" style=\"--accent:var(--aws-magenta);\" data-correct=\"A\" data-multi=\"\" data-rationale=\"X-Ray provides end-to-end tracing across API Gateway, Lambda, and DynamoDB. Enabling X-Ray on API Gateway and Lambda captures the time spent at each service. Instrumenting DynamoDB calls with the X-Ray SDK creates subsegments showing individual call latency. The service map visualizes the request flow and identifies bottlenecks.\" data-judge=\"{&quot;prompt&quot;: &quot;Question 4: finding which hop is costing the time&quot;, &quot;options&quot;: [&quot;Enable AWS X-Ray tracing on API Gateway and the Lambda function. Use the X-Ray SDK in the Lambda function code to instrument downstream DynamoDB calls. View the service map and traces in the X-Ray console.&quot;, &quot;Enable CloudWatch detailed monitoring for all services. Create a CloudWatch dashboard with latency metrics for API Gateway, Lambda, and DynamoDB.&quot;, &quot;Enable API Gateway access logging and Lambda function logging. Use CloudWatch Logs Insights to query timestamps across log groups and calculate durations.&quot;, &quot;Enable DynamoDB Streams on the table. Create a Lambda function that calculates read latency from the stream records and publishes metrics to CloudWatch.&quot;], &quot;answer&quot;: &quot;Enable AWS X-Ray tracing on API Gateway and the Lambda function. Use the X-Ray SDK in the Lambda function code to instrument downstream DynamoDB calls. View the service map and traces in the X-Ray console.&quot;, &quot;why&quot;: &quot;X-Ray provides end-to-end tracing across API Gateway, Lambda, and DynamoDB. Enabling X-Ray on API Gateway and Lambda captures the time spent at each service. Instrumenting DynamoDB calls with the X-Ray SDK creates subsegments showing individual call latency. The service map visualizes the request flow and identifies bottlenecks.&quot;, &quot;explanations&quot;: {&quot;A&quot;: &quot;X-Ray provides end-to-end tracing across API Gateway, Lambda, and DynamoDB. Enabling X-Ray on API Gateway and Lambda captures the time spent at each service. Instrumenting DynamoDB calls with the X-Ray SDK creates subsegments showing individual call latency. The service map visualizes the request flow and identifies bottlenecks.&quot;, &quot;B&quot;: &quot;CloudWatch metrics show aggregate latency per service but do not trace individual requests through the system. A dashboard shows averages and percentiles but cannot correlate a single slow API request to a specific slow DynamoDB call within that request. Distributed tracing requires X-Ray.&quot;, &quot;C&quot;: &quot;Access logs and function logs record timestamps that can be correlated manually. However, this requires writing complex queries and manually calculating durations between components. This approach does not provide a visual service map or automatic trace correlation. X-Ray provides this functionality natively with minimal effort.&quot;, &quot;D&quot;: &quot;DynamoDB Streams capture data modification events (inserts, updates, deletes). They do not record read operations or their latency. Streams cannot help identify which DynamoDB reads are slow. Additionally, this approach only monitors DynamoDB and does not provide end-to-end request tracing.&quot;}, &quot;sources&quot;: [&quot;https://docs.aws.amazon.com/xray/latest/devguide/aws-xray.html&quot;, &quot;https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/services-xray.html&quot;]}\" data-sources=\"[&quot;https://docs.aws.amazon.com/xray/latest/devguide/aws-xray.html&quot;, &quot;https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/services-xray.html&quot;]\"><div class=\"kicker\">SESSION 5 &middot; KNOWLEDGE CHECK &middot; QUESTION 4 of 7</div><h2>Check yourself <span class=\"sm-check-hint\">click an option</span></h2><p class=\"sm-check-q\">Question 4: finding which hop is costing the time</p><div class=\"sm-opts\"><button class=\"sm-opt\" data-ltr=\"A\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"sm-opt-l\">A</span><span class=\"sm-opt-t\">Enable AWS X-Ray tracing on API Gateway and the Lambda function. Use the X-Ray SDK in the Lambda function code to instrument downstream DynamoDB calls. View the service map and traces in the X-Ray console.</span><span class=\"sm-opt-m\"></span></button><button class=\"sm-opt\" data-ltr=\"B\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"sm-opt-l\">B</span><span class=\"sm-opt-t\">Enable CloudWatch detailed monitoring for all services. Create a CloudWatch dashboard with latency metrics for API Gateway, Lambda, and DynamoDB.</span><span class=\"sm-opt-m\"></span></button><button class=\"sm-opt\" data-ltr=\"C\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"sm-opt-l\">C</span><span class=\"sm-opt-t\">Enable API Gateway access logging and Lambda function logging. Use CloudWatch Logs Insights to query timestamps across log groups and calculate durations.</span><span class=\"sm-opt-m\"></span></button><button class=\"sm-opt\" data-ltr=\"D\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"sm-opt-l\">D</span><span class=\"sm-opt-t\">Enable DynamoDB Streams on the table. Create a Lambda function that calculates read latency from the stream records and publishes metrics to CloudWatch.</span><span class=\"sm-opt-m\"></span></button></div><div class=\"sm-check-fb\" style=\"display:none\"></div><div class=\"sm-source\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-circle-question\"></i> Knowledge check &middot; Content Review Session 5</div></div>",
